<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  THE camera mounting pose. Single source of truth, included by both camera
  descriptions so sim and hardware cannot drift apart.

    rx150_gripper_depth_camera.urdf.xacro   sim, Gazebo depth sensor
    rx150_realsense_camera.urdf.xacro       hardware, RealSense driver

  Both bolt their camera link onto rx150/gripper_link with this pose, and both
  mean the same physical point - the camera's own reference origin, which for a
  D435 sits a few mm inside the casing behind the front glass.

  xyz is MEASURED and DERIVED here, never pasted in. rpy is NOT measured; see
  the warning at the bottom.

  ===========================================================================
  Where xyz comes from
  ===========================================================================
  A datum on the robot, plus three tape measurements from that datum to the
  camera. Both live below as properties and cam_mount_xyz is computed from
  them, so there is no hand-summed number to fall out of step.

  THE DATUM is mount_ref_corner - the top front corner of the LEFT face of the
  square wrist servo housing, the corner closest to the left finger. Read off
  the mesh, not estimated: the left face is the plane y = 0.01425 (half the
  28.5 mm housing width) and spans x 0.000 to 0.0430, so the corner is
  [0.04300, 0.01425, 0.03525] in gripper_link. The sim description draws a
  green sphere there (show_mount_ref, on by default) so it can be eyeballed in
  Gazebo and RViz before any number here is trusted.

  An earlier datum was a screw hole guessed 6 mm back and 6 mm down from this
  corner, because the sim mesh has no screw holes. A corner is an edge a tape
  can be held against and a hole was not, which is why it moved.

  THE MEASUREMENTS are named for the physical direction they were taken in,
  not x/y/z. This matters: a previous round handed over three bare magnitudes
  labelled "x, y, z" without recording which physical axis each one named, and
  four different readings of the same three numbers were all defensible. Never
  record this measurement as x/y/z again.

  They were taken FROM the depth camera LENS FACE TO the corner, and are
  negated below into the corner-to-camera offsets the joint needs. The lens
  face is not the depth origin, so cam_lens_to_depth_origin backs the depth
  measurement off to it; do not apply that correction a second time anywhere.

    lateral   24.55 mm, corner is LEFT of the lens, so the camera sits that
              far toward the RIGHT finger of the corner. This is what puts the
              camera slightly right of the gripper centreline - the D435's
              left imager is off-centre in its casing.
    depth      3.26 mm, corner is BEHIND the lens, so the camera sits that far
              FORWARD of the corner. Note this is SMALLER than the lens-to-
              depth-origin backoff below, so the depth origin ends up just
              BEHIND the corner (cam_off_x is negative, about -0.9 mm).
    height    18.76 mm, corner is BELOW the lens, so the camera sits that far
              ABOVE the corner.

  These replace a first set taken before the bracket was re-squared. That set
  read 25.44 / 14.49 / 18.89 in the same three directions; with it the arm
  landed about 22 mm to the RIGHT of the cup, which at the 0.44-0.57 m
  detection range works out to 2-3 deg of camera YAW. The bracket was adjusted
  to remove that yaw, which moved the camera and forced this re-measurement.
  The directions did not change, only the magnitudes.

  To re-measure, replace only the three magnitudes below, after checking each
  direction word still describes what the tape did.

  ===========================================================================
  rpy IS STILL UNMEASURED
  ===========================================================================
  YAW has now been dealt with in HARDWARE, not here: the bracket was physically
  re-squared after a 22 mm lateral miss showed 2-3 deg of yaw, which is why the
  measurements above are a second set. So 0 for yaw is now a deliberate zero.

  ROLL and PITCH are still unchecked, and 0 0 0 asserts the camera is mounted
  perfectly square to the gripper in those axes too. It is the term that
  matters most for the obstacle map: a
  rotation error does not shift the map, it SMEARS it, because each sweep
  station views the scene from a different angle and lands the same surface in
  a different place. 3 deg of pitch is about 26 mm of height error at 0.5 m,
  on its own enough to lift the work surface above the sweep's z_min crop and
  have the planner route around the table as if it were a wall.

  If the map still comes out thick with a correct xyz, measure this next.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- The datum. Mesh geometry, not a measurement. -->
  <xacro:property name="mount_ref_x" value="0.04300" scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="mount_ref_y" value="0.01425" scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="mount_ref_z" value="0.03525" scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="mount_ref_xyz"
                  value="${mount_ref_x} ${mount_ref_y} ${mount_ref_z}"
                  scope="global"/>

  <!-- Tape, lens face to corner. Magnitudes only; the direction words are in
       the header and decide the signs below. -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_meas_lateral" value="0.02455"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_meas_depth"   value="0.00326"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_meas_height"  value="0.01876"/>

  <!-- D435 depth start point, 4.2 mm behind the front cover glass. -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_lens_to_depth_origin" value="0.0042"/>

  <!-- Corner to camera. Negated, because the tape ran the other way. -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_off_x" value="${ cam_meas_depth - cam_lens_to_depth_origin}"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_off_y" value="${-cam_meas_lateral}"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_off_z" value="${ cam_meas_height}"/>

  <!-- Rounded because binary floating point turns 0.03525 + 0.01889 into
       0.054139999999999994, which is the same number and much worse to read in
       a rendered URDF or a TF dump. -->
  <xacro:property name="cam_mount_xyz"
                  value="${round(mount_ref_x + cam_off_x, 6)} ${round(mount_ref_y + cam_off_y, 6)} ${round(mount_ref_z + cam_off_z, 6)}"
                  scope="global"/>
  <xacro:property name="cam_mount_rpy" value="0 0 0" scope="global"/>
</robot>
